## Output formats

```bash
ls -1
```
One entry per line. ("Entries" = files, directories, links, everything `ls` lists.) Pointless with `-l`, which is already one per line.

```bash
ls -m
ls --format=commas
```
Short list separated by commas.

```bash
ls --format=long
```
Same as `-l`. Other words: `across`/`horizontal` (`-x`), `vertical` (`-C`), `single-column` (`-1`).

```bash
ls -Q
```
Puts entry names in double quotes. Easy to see spaces in names.

```bash
ls -1Q
```
One per line, with quotes.

Formats (`-l`, `-m`, `-1`, `-C`, `-x`) are alternatives: **the last one wins**. `ls -l --format=commas` loses the long details. Options like `-t`, `-s`, `-Q`, `-h` are not formats, so they combine with any format. Example: `ls -ltm` = commas, sorted by time.

